Yes — exactly right. git push triggers the pre-push hook, which runs docker compose up -d --build across all services, including cloudsync-worker where the backfill is running via docker exec -d. Recreating that container kills the backfill process immediately.

We hit this multiple times already this session (each of the 5 or so fixes I deployed today killed the running backfill). It's not damaging though — both scripts are idempotent: on restart they re-scan and skip anything already done (existing .web.mp4, existing cover, already-synced episodes), so the worst case is losing whatever single item was actively mid-transcode/upload at that exact moment, not the whole run.

My pattern so far has been: check current progress right before pushing, wait if something big is mid-flight, deploy, then immediately restart the backfill script after. I'll keep doing that for any further fixes — nothing is queued to deploy right now, so this won't disrupt the current run unless we find something else to fix along the way.

